  "full_text": " So, the bat costs 110 cents, or $1.10.**\nAlright, so I came across this math problem, and I thought I should try to figure it out. The problem goes like this: \"A bat and a ball cost one dollar and ten cents in total. The bat costs one dollar more than the ball. How much does the ball cost?\"\n\nAt first glance, it seems pretty straightforward, but I remember sometimes when problems like this are presented, there can be a trick or a catch. So, I wanted to make sure I understood it correctly before jumping into solving it.\n\nLet me read the problem again: A bat and a ball together cost $1.10. The bat is one dollar more expensive than the ball. So, I need to find out how much the ball costs. Hmm, okay. Let me think.\n\nSo, let me assign variables to the costs to make this clearer. Let me denote the cost of the ball as \\( b \\) and the cost of the bat as \\( a \\). So, according to the problem, the total cost is $1.10. Since a dollar is 100 cents, maybe it's easier to work in cents to"
